WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:03.990 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  a protest that's been days in the making  thousands of people gather on the  streets of hartoum demanding a return to  transitional government something they  say was robbed from them after the  military coup on monday  we went to the street because we reject  the military rule and we reject  authoritarianism and we categorically  reject all the behavior of the military  council  we want to topple the security committee  and bring it down we don't trust these  people this is not their first coup we  need the world to know that the sudanese  people are living under oppression and  are being beaten our voices are not  listened to because there is no coverage  and there is no internet  they're risking a lot  days of protests in the capital led to  street to street battles with the  military and demonstrators killed with  the internet cut off organizers used  masks flyers and graffiti to reach  people  the military also blocked the main road  to the airport the planned route of this  march saturday's protests were met with  tear gas and live ammunition what is  really concerning is the vast you know  array of military and security actors  that are on the streets there are just  incredible set of of of security sectors  that now patrol the streets um  no sense that they are necessarily  taking orders from a central spot and i  think that um you know that diversity of  different military actors is really the  worry  these demonstrators are demanding that  prime minister abdullah's cabinet be  restored it was toppled by general abdel  fattah al-burhan  he also dissolved the sovereignty  council established after the overthrow  of umar al-bashir two years ago some  political parties have supported the  coup  burhan says he will hold elections in  july 2023 and then hand power to the  elected civilian government  but the takeover has provoked  international condemnation the un and  u.s has called on the military not to  intervene in the protests  regional and international actors have  been urging general burhan to reverse  his school and restore the transitional  government which he dissolved on monday  but many protesters here say even if  that happens it still falls short of  their demands to completely hand over  power to civilian rules  the people on khartoum streets call it a  power grab and demand a return to a  democratic process heber morgan  al-jazeera horton

